# ── Step 229: theme toggle's @media(prefers-color-scheme:dark) block was unguarded against an explicit data-theme="light" choice — a recurring, previously-flagged contract violation ──

:step-themeToggleOsPreferenceGuard a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 229 ;
    schema:name "Any @media(prefers-color-scheme:dark) :root block MUST be guarded with :not([data-theme=\"light\"]) (or the dark-first equivalent, :not([data-theme=\"dark\"]) on a light media query) — an unguarded block repaints over an explicit opposite choice, so the toggle silently does nothing for any visitor whose OS preference disagrees with the click"@en ;
    schema:text "2026-08-19: user said this exact class of bug 'has been covered multiple times now as a contract violation' — found via live browser testing (OS color-scheme emulation + real click) that generate_infographic.py's own scripts/templates/styles.css and base_template.html shipped the unguarded form despite a correctly-guarded version already existing in a different template in the same skill; also hardcoded <html data-theme=\"light\"> plus a toggle ternary that silently no-ops on a first click when the attribute is absent — full incident and the three-part fix in the howto." ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerBehaviorGapIdentified, onto:triggerUserCorrection ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <howto/theme-toggle-os-preference-guard.ttl> .

# ── Step 230: never write a literal star-slash inside a CSS comment — it closes the comment early and silently kills the whole following rule ──

:step-cssCommentStarSlashTrap a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 230 ;
    schema:name "A CSS comment documenting custom-property names must never contain a literal star-slash (as in an --opal-*/--ols-* glob) — CSS has no comment nesting or escaping, so the first star-slash terminates the comment and the remaining prose is parsed as CSS, silently discarding the :root block that follows"@en ;
    schema:text "2026-08-19: writing a palette-provenance comment above :root, the glob '--opal-*/--ols-*' closed the comment at '--opal-*/', which dropped every custom property and rendered the page black-on-white with no hero gradient and an invisible gradient-clipped heading; caught only because a live screenshot looked wrong, since grep on the file showed the intended text present — see the howto for the detection method and the balance-checker to run after editing any generated CSS." ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerBehaviorGapIdentified, onto:triggerArtifactGeneration ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <howto/css-comment-star-slash-trap.ttl> .

# ── Step 231: the OpenLink brand palette was a recorded standing default but the generator template never implemented it ──

:step-openlinkPaletteInGeneratorTemplate a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 231 ;
    schema:name "rdf-infographic-skill/scripts/templates/styles.css now ships the OpenLink brand palette Step 125 declared the standing default back on 2026-07-13, plus --border-strong / --accent-soft / --on-accent tokens; a standing default recorded only in memory but absent from the generator is not actually in force"@en ;
    schema:text "2026-08-19: a generated page was still emitting the generic indigo/cyan placeholder palette 5 weeks after Step 125 made the OpenLink scheme the default, because the rule lived in preferences.ttl while the template kept the old literals — the third generator-vs-recorded-rule contradiction found in one session, alongside Steps 228 and 229." ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerBehaviorGapIdentified, onto:triggerArtifactGeneration ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <howto/openlink-brand-color-scheme.ttl>,
        <preferences.ttl#step-openlinkBrandColorScheme>,
        <howto/generator-script-output-not-a-substitute-for-contract-check.ttl> .

# ── Step 232: hand-authored HTML derived from RDF drifts out of sync the moment the RDF changes — generate it, don't hand-patch it ──

:step-tierMapDriftFromHandAuthoring a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 232 ;
    schema:name "A tier/category map (or any HTML section presenting an RDF-derived count or list) MUST be regenerated from the graph whenever the graph changes underneath it, not hand-patched — a hand-written panel silently disagreed with its own source TTL until the user asked a direct question about tier counts"@en ;
    schema:text "2026-08-19: after adding a new sample workload instance to the TTL, the already-rendered tier-map HTML kept its old per-tier counts (3/3/5 instead of 4/3/5) because it had been hand-authored as static markup rather than generated from a query; caught only when the user asked whether the Threshold-Crossing and Context-and-Execution tiers had real instance data, prompting a walk of the graph that surfaced the mismatch — fixed by rewriting the section as a small Python/rdflib pass over the companion TTL." ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerBehaviorGapIdentified, onto:triggerArtifactGeneration ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <howto/thesis-framework-openlink-brand-template.ttl> .

# ── Step 233: the OpenLink-brand thesis/framework template is the standing preferred default for kg-generator Thesis & Framework Article content ──

:step-thesisFrameworkTemplatePreference a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 233 ;
    schema:name "assets/templates/thesis-framework-openlink-brand-claude_opus_5.html is the PREFERRED DEFAULT template for Thesis & Framework Article content going forward — select it before other templates unless the user names a different one"@en ;
    schema:text "2026-08-19: after iterating a Thesis & Framework Article page (OpenLink brand palette, RDF-generated tier map, typed evidence claims, demo SPARQL queries proving the sample data, comment-thread cards) through several rounds of user-caught gaps, the user asked for this look and the underlying data-completeness discipline to become the standing default rather than a one-off — recorded as a template in rdf-infographic-skill plus the required-adaptations checklist in template-options.md." ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerUserCorrection, onto:triggerArtifactGeneration ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <howto/thesis-framework-openlink-brand-template.ttl>,
        <../references/template-options.md> .


# ── Step 234: the post's author is whoever the publishing platform asserts — additional names in body byline text are mentions, never credited contributor roles ──

:step-authorVsBodyBylineNames a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 234 ;
    schema:name "schema:author = the single author of record the publishing platform asserts; extra names in the article's own \"By: X, Y, Z\" line are schema:contributor and MUST stay visible by rendering that byline verbatim — but never as a second, separate \"Contributors:\" credit line"@en ;
    schema:text "2026-08-19: settled after three rounds on one byline — schema:author is the single platform-asserted author of record, the article's own byline is rendered verbatim (so co-writers stay visible), those co-writers plus the commenters are schema:contributor, and no separate 'Contributors:' credit line is added; see the howto for the correction history and the half-fix / over-correction failure modes." ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerUserCorrection, onto:triggerArtifactGeneration ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <howto/author-vs-body-byline-names.ttl>,
        <howto/thesis-framework-openlink-brand-template.ttl> .


# ── Step 236: Editorial data-viz infographic aesthetic (preferred default, 2026-08-19) ──

:step-editorialDataVizAesthetic a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 236 ;
    schema:name "Editorial data-viz infographic aesthetic (preferred HTML doc aesthetic going forward)"@en ;
    schema:text """The HTML aesthetic of the Right-Sizing Your Intelligence Spend collection (DeepSeek V4 Flash, 2026-08-19, user-approved after review) is the preferred default HTML document aesthetic going forward for thesis/framework and data-rich collections, unless the user names a different template or an existing artifact's visual language must be preserved. Signature components: (1) CSS-variable theming — light/dark with html[data-theme="dark"] and @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) as two separate blocks, no hardcoded colors; (2) editorial typography with uppercase letter-spaced eyebrows, strong display headings, muted section-sub lines; (3) inline-SVG data visualizations as the signature — a hero threshold/capability curve (rising line with a shaded 'frontier band' and workload dots positioned by threshold), a threshold spectrum bar with a rising capability line, a workload intelligence map (scatter of sample instances by intelligence-threshold x token-sensitivity, colored by value class, with a shaded frontier-reserve region), and a descending 'intelligence per successful outcome' trend line; (4) stat cards with mini bars; (5) animated line draws via stroke-dashoffset (disabled under prefers-reduced-motion); (6) responsive dual-presentation comparison matrices (table >=901px, cards <=900px, resolver-linked aspect columns); (7) every visual dot/entity resolver-linked to its KG IRI (describe/?url=). The full harness contract remains mandatory: floating collapsed nav, theme toggle in the header, KG Explorer (Basic/Advanced, settings panel, sticky drag, click-guard resolver), SPARQL workbench with demo instance data visible, 8-item attribution footer, GATE: validate-harness-contract.py 0 failures before delivery. Canonical implementation: {LLM_ROOT}/DeepSeek/_build/right-sizing/html_{css,visuals,body,js}.py and webpages/right-sizing-intelligence-spend-deepseek_v4flash-1.html; full spec: howto/editorial-data-viz-aesthetic.ttl."""@en ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerArtifactGeneration, onto:triggerUserCorrection ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <howto/editorial-data-viz-aesthetic.ttl>,
        <../../../../LLMs/DeepSeek/webpages/right-sizing-intelligence-spend-deepseek_v4flash-1.html> .

# ── Step 238: Hyperlink generated SPARQL URLs ──

:step-hyperlinkSparqlUrls a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 238 ;
    schema:name "Whenever a SPARQL query URL is generated, present it as a clickable hyperlink — never as bare unlinked text"@en ;
    schema:text "2026-08-19: user directive after the llm-routing-skill SPARQL endpoint URL was shown as a long raw string. Rule: every generated SPARQL query URL MUST be emitted as a Markdown/HTML hyperlink with a short human label (e.g. 'Run: code-generation frontier (cheapest first)'), and the URL must be verified to return results before it is presented. FORMAT: SELECT SPARQL URLs use the data-twingler convention — format=text/x-html+tr (percent-encoded as text%2Fx-html%2Btr, since '+' in a raw URL decodes to space), e.g. http://localhost:8890/sparql?default-graph-uri=&query=...&format=text%2Fx-html%2Btr — NOT format=json. Applies to chat responses, generated documents, and skill output alike. See llm-routing-skill SKILL.md SPARQL Query Templates for the query corpus this rule governs."@en ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerPreferencesUpdate ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <howto/hyperlink-sparql-urls.ttl> .

# ── Step 239: no generated skill ships a secret literal — resolve via credential store, prompt last ──

:step-secretResolutionInGeneratedSkills a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 239 ;
    schema:name "A skill/script/example this agent authors or packages MUST NOT contain a secret literal; secrets resolve through the four-source chain (explicit arg → env var → OS credential store → hidden prompt), and 'it's only a throwaway testnet credential' is not an exemption"@en ;
    schema:text "2026-08-19: while generating the x402-buyer skill from a user-supplied zip, the source x402_get.py carried a Base Sepolia buyer private key in its docstring, deliberately committed so the smoke test could be rerun. Flagged at delivery; the user asked for it to be moved to the Keychain and prompted for when required. Generalises p12-passphrase-retrieval.ttl's chain from the one standing PKCS#12 passphrase to any secret in any generated artifact, and adds the migration ordering (verify free → add → verify readback → THEN strip from source) plus the pre-package grep sweep over both the skill directory and the built ZIP." ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerArtifactGeneration, onto:triggerSkillInvocation, onto:triggerUserCorrection ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <howto/secret-resolution-in-generated-skills.ttl>,
        <howto/p12-passphrase-retrieval.ttl#step-keychainPrimary>,
        <howto/p12-passphrase-retrieval.ttl#step-neverProbeProductionCredentialNames>,
        <howto/p12-passphrase-retrieval.ttl#step-redact>,
        <preferences.ttl#step-p12PassphraseKeychainFirst> .

# ── Step 240: WebID-TLS/NetID-TLS always defaults to port 5443 on OpenLink resource servers ──

:step-webidTlsPort5443Default a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 240 ;
    schema:name "WebID-TLS/NetID-TLS targets port 5443 automatically, as an upfront default, never discovered after a misleading 401 on port 443"@en ;
    schema:text "2026-08-19: live-verified against ods-qa.openlinksw.com — port 443 never issues a CertificateRequest during the TLS handshake (falls straight to 401 Digest regardless of cert availability); port 5443 does, and the resulting 302 ?k=... redirect precedes the real 402/challenge. Applied to x402-buyer (URL port auto-rewrite via rewrite_for_webid_tls(), plus new --cert client-cert support that was entirely absent before — the script could not do WebID-TLS at all) and acp-client (promoted from buried Protocol-Specific-Notes prose to an upfront blockquote RULE in Auth Protocol Selection). Rewrite fires only when the URL is on port 443 or has no explicit port; an explicit non-443 port is left alone as a deliberate override." ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerUserCorrection, onto:triggerArtifactGeneration ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <howto/webid-tls-port-5443-default.ttl>,
        <../x402-buyer/SKILL.md>,
        <../acp-client/SKILL.md> .

# ── Step 241: BEGIN{IGNORECASE=1} is a gawk-only extension — silently non-functional on macOS's BSD awk ──

:step-awkIgnorecaseNotPortable a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 241 ;
    schema:name "Never rely on awk's IGNORECASE variable for case-insensitive matching in a shell pipeline meant to run cross-platform — use grep -i instead"@en ;
    schema:text "2026-08-19: found live-testing the x402-buyer WebID-TLS probe flow — awk 'BEGIN{IGNORECASE=1}/^payment-required:/{...}' had been silently non-functional on macOS since first written. BSD/nawk awk (macOS's default) treats IGNORECASE as an ordinary uninitialized variable with no special meaning; the match stays case-sensitive and never fires against curl's uppercase PAYMENT-REQUIRED: header. No error — just an empty result that reads exactly like 'no such header present,' a false negative for a real, present header. Fixed by replacing with grep -i '^header:' | cut -d: -f2- | tr -d ' \\r' — no GNU-awk dependency. This is a general authoring rule, not a one-off fix: audit any awk BEGIN{IGNORECASE=1} usage authored for a skill meant to run on a user's actual machine." ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerBehaviorGapIdentified, onto:triggerArtifactGeneration ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <howto/webid-tls-port-5443-default.ttl#step-awkIgnorecaseBugFoundEnRoute> .

# ── Step 242: bash 3.2's set -u errors expanding an empty array — use the ${ARR[@]+"${ARR[@]}"} idiom ──

:step-bash32EmptyArrayIdiom a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 242 ;
    schema:name "Any shell script with set -u and an optional/conditionally-empty array must expand it as ${ARR[@]+\"${ARR[@]}\"}, never bare \"${ARR[@]}\" — bash 3.2 (macOS's shipped default) errors \"unbound variable\" on the bare form when the array is empty"@en ;
    schema:text "2026-08-19: introduced in x402-buyer/examples/probe-challenge.sh's --cert support and caught within the same session — the array-populated (cert-supplied) path worked, but the more common empty-array (no-cert, plain probe) path failed outright before any curl request ran. Confirmed as a genuine bash <4.4 nounset quirk, not a typo; macOS ships bash 3.2 as /bin/bash regardless of Homebrew bash being available. Always test a script's own default/no-flag path explicitly, not just the path that was being actively developed." ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerBehaviorGapIdentified, onto:triggerArtifactGeneration ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <howto/webid-tls-port-5443-default.ttl#step-bash32EmptyArrayBug> .

# ── Step 243: the WebID-TLS redirect-to-402 hop is not universal — some resource types answer 402 directly ──

:step-webidTlsRedirectNotUniversal a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 243 ;
    schema:name "Do not assume every WebID-TLS-gated x402 resource 302-redirects through a ?k=... URL before its 402 — a /sparql/ query endpoint answered 402 directly at :5443 with no redirect at all"@en ;
    schema:text "2026-08-19: confirmed with a live probe of an ods-qa.openlinksw.com SPARQL query endpoint (urn:jch:test named graph) — cert accepted on :5443 went straight to 402, no 302/?k= hop, unlike the DAV file resource probed earlier in the same session. A redirect-follow (curl -L) handles both shapes correctly as a no-op when nothing needs following; don't build detection logic that treats an absent redirect as a failure signal." ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerArtifactGeneration ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <howto/webid-tls-port-5443-default.ttl#step-redirectHopNotUniversal> .

# ── Step 244: x402 Python SDK requires jsonschema even for a plain fetch — validates server-offered extensions before signing ──

:step-x402JsonschemaRequired a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 244 ;
    schema:name "The x402 Python SDK needs jsonschema + the extensions extra even when the caller uses no extensions — it validates whatever extension the SERVER'S challenge advertises before creating a payment payload"@en ;
    schema:text "2026-08-19: discovered running x402-buyer's first real end-to-end settlement. A challenge advertising eip2612GasSponsoring failed deep inside signing with ImportError: Extensions validation requires jsonschema — after the challenge had already decoded and the buyer address already printed, reading like a mid-run crash rather than a missing dependency. Fixed in every install line across the skill (docstring, SKILL.md, setup.md) plus a troubleshooting.md entry keyed on the exact error text." ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerArtifactGeneration, onto:triggerBehaviorGapIdentified ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <howto/webid-tls-port-5443-default.ttl#step-jsonschemaRequiredDependency> .

# ── Step 245: verify a payment client's own success report independently against the chain before repeating it ──

:step-verifySettlementIndependently a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 245 ;
    schema:name "When a payment/settlement client reports success, verify independently against the underlying ledger before repeating the claim — never take the client's self-report as sufficient when real value (even testnet) moved"@en ;
    schema:text "2026-08-19: x402-buyer's first real settlement (ods-qa.openlinksw.com TestFile.txt, $9.99) reported success with a transaction hash. Verified independently via a direct eth_getTransactionReceipt RPC call to the SAME already-established Base Sepolia endpoint the script itself defaults to (never a fresh unverified URL) — confirmed status 0x1, correct ERC-20 Transfer event, correct from/to/amount matching the challenge exactly. A block-explorer URL (sepolia.basescan.org) was tried first, returned 403, and was NOT worked around with a substitute or fabricated URL — fell back to the already-verified RPC endpoint instead, per the no-fabricated-URLs rule. Also checked remaining wallet balance directly via balanceOf() rather than assuming from the settlement amount." ;
    onto:hasTrigger onto:triggerBehaviorGapIdentified, onto:triggerArtifactGeneration ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <howto/webid-tls-port-5443-default.ttl#step-firstRealSettlementVerified> .
